-//! Bounded structural recording for one generic linear-combination
-//! shape produced through `NativeChip::configure` /
-//! `NativeChip::linear_combination` synthesis.
-//!
-//! Opt-in only: nothing is recorded unless a caller holds a guard
-//! from [`begin_recording`] on the current thread across a production
-//! call (`keygen_vk_with_k`, `keygen_pk`, or `create_proof`).
-//! Production entry points never arm recording themselves. While no
-//! session is active, each hook performs one thread-local check and
-//! returns; no values are encoded, cloned, or constructed for
-//! recording, and the floor-planner hook does not invoke the
-//! region-name closure.
-//!
-//! # Supported subset, stated exactly
-//!
-//! The joined view in this module is a **generic
-//! linear-combination-shaped structural view**. It is not an
-//! established `NativeAdd` view: the recorded gate fixed encodings
-//! are opaque `PrimeField::to_repr` byte strings, and this module
-//! does not validate that they equal the add pattern
-//! `(-1, 1, 1, 0, 0)` with zero constant, `q_next`, `mul_ab`, and
-//! `mul_ac`. Deriving an actual `NativeAdd` — or any accepted
-//! assignment correspondence — from this view remains open and is
-//! not delivered here.
-//!
-//! A join can succeed only for sessions in which all of the
-//! following hold, and refuses otherwise:
-//!
-//! - the ordinary multi-term `linear_combination` path actually
-//!   created one `"Linear combination"` region (the empty-terms
-//!   early return to `assign_fixed(constant)`, the singleton
-//!   coefficient-`1`/zero-constant identity early return, and the
-//!   chip's cached-zero path create no such region and refuse at
-//!   join with `RegionMismatch`);
-//! - the zero term actually created an `"Assign fixed"` region
-//!   rather than being served only from the `cached_fixed` cache,
-//!   and neither add operand was filtered out as the cached zero;
-//! - both phases were laid out by the recording-aware single-pass
-//!   floor planner (`SingleChipLayouter`); other floor planners do
-//!   not call the region hook and refuse at join;
-//! - no other operation created additional distinct `"Assign
-//!   fixed"` or `"Linear combination"` bases, and no second distinct
-//!   `NativeChip` binding was configured under the same guard.
-//!
-//! # Armed-mode behaviour (not behaviour-preserving in general)
-//!
-//! While a guard is armed on a thread,
-//! `SingleChipLayouter::assign_region` invokes the region-name
-//! closure `name()` once extra for **every** region of **every**
-//! circuit laid out on that thread — not only for `"Assign fixed"`
-//! and `"Linear combination"`. The name filter runs only after the
-//! closure has been called. Armed use therefore requires pure,
-//! idempotent region-name closures: a closure that panics, mutates
-//! interior state, is non-idempotent, or reenters recording is
-//! unsupported while armed, and arming is not behaviour-preserving
-//! for arbitrary circuits. Likewise, any `NativeChip::configure`
-//! call on the armed thread can contribute a binding, including
-//! configure-only or key-reading contexts that synthesize nothing.
-//!
-//! # Recording, retention, and privacy
-//!
-//! Recording captures the full keygen fixed/selector tables and the
-//! merged proving advice columns for the synthesized circuit, not
-//! only the final joined cells. Finished sessions are retained in
-//! the `LAST_KEYGEN` / `LAST_PROVING` thread-locals — complete
-//! recorded tables, including proving witness advice encodings —
-//! until they are taken, replaced by a later session on the same
-//! thread, or the thread exits. Any code running on the same thread
-//! can call the public [`take_last_keygen_recording`] /
-//! [`take_last_proving_recording`] functions; thread reuse is not a
-//! privacy boundary. Callers must take sessions promptly.
-//! `Debug` for [`RecordedSession`] and
-//! [`JoinedLinearCombinationView`] is redacted to dimensions and
-//! counts and never prints recorded byte tables.
-//!
-//! # Caller-declared labels
-//!
-//! [`RecordedSession::declared_phase`] and
-//! [`RecordedSession::declared_circuit_count`] are supplied by the
-//! caller of [`begin_recording`]. They are not measured from any
-//! production call: a guard can span zero calls, several calls, or
-//! a call of the opposite phase. [`join_linear_combination_view`]
-//! checks only that both declarations equal `1`; it does not prove
-//! that exactly one matching synthesis happened, and taking a
-//! session proves nothing about which production call produced it.
-//!
-//! # Guard lifecycle
-//!
-//! The guard is not `Send` and not `Sync`: it must be created,
-//! held, and dropped on the thread that called [`begin_recording`].
-//! Guards may nest. Dropping in LIFO order finalizes and publishes
-//! each session. Dropping a guard out of order removes only that
-//! guard's own session (which is discarded, not published), leaves
-//! any inner active session untouched and still recording, and
-//! never resurrects the dropped session. `Drop` never panics,
-//! including during TLS teardown or unwinding; a guard must not be
-//! dropped from inside a recording callback while the recorder is
-//! borrowed by that callback's own hook.
-//!
-//! # Reentrancy and panic semantics of hooks
-//!
-//! No hook holds the thread-local borrow across a caller callback,
-//! field evaluation or encoding, or `Debug` formatting. Each hook
-//! reads the active session id, releases the borrow, performs its
-//! external work, then stores into the frame with that id. A
-//! callback may therefore call other hooks or begin and end a
-//! nested session without a `RefCell` borrow panic; if the
-//! originating session ended during the external work, the produced
-//! value is discarded. Region-name closures are the caller's code
-//! and run under the caller's own contract above.
-//!
-//! Recorded sessions are plain public data and are
-//! caller-constructible. The join is therefore a shape-agreement
-//! check over recorded fields only. It does not authenticate
-//! provenance, bind a verifying or proving key, identify a concrete
-//! circuit, state that any gate or copy constraint is satisfied,
-//! state that a verifier accepts anything, or state that the byte
-//! encoding is a homomorphism into any other carrier.
